  "query_preview": "..\nAs of 230 PM EST Thursday...\n\nKEY MESSAGE 1...Above normal temperatures and tranquil weather \nthrough Saturday.\n\nHigh pressure remains overhead allowing for mild days and \nseasonably cool night. Some high clouds filtering into the area \nin the SW flow aloft will likely keep overnight lows tonight \nhigher than they've been the past few days. \n\nKEY MESSAGE 2...Ensemble guidance showing increasing chance for\nrain and gusty winds on Sunday, especially close to the coast.\n\nAs we've been monitoring for the last few days, low pressure is\nexpected to develop along the stationary frontal boundary \nacross Florida and the Bahamas. Still somewhat lower confidence \nfor the eventual intensity and track of expected coastal low \ndevelopment for the weekend due to the uncertainty of where \nalong the front the low will develop. However, the super- \nensemble of the 12z GEFS, 06z EPS and 06z AI-EPS suggest a \npotential low track a little closer to the coast. The \ndeterministic runs of the 12z GFS and 12z REFS show a strong low\nmoving onshore in the Carolinas, while the 12z ECMWF shows a \nslower and weaker low moving toward the Carolina coastline, then\nmoving northeast by Sunday night. \n\nAlthough the eventual track is still uncertain, there is \nsomewhat increased confidence that the low will be close enough \nto the region to allow for increased rain chances across the \nentire area. The best chance for rain will still be in eastern \nVA/NE NC, as the probabilities for 1\" of rainfall across \nespecially areas east of I- 95 have increased to 50+ percent in \nthe latest NBM guidance. With that said, have increased PoPs \nacross the entire area on Sunday. There are still some scenarios\nwhich the area sees very little rainfall, but those scenarios \nare seemingly less likely than yesterday. \n\nAs for other impacts, there could be a brief period of gusty NE\nto SE winds along the coast on Sunday, but the system will be \nmoving fast enough to limit any significant impacts. In \naddition, given that the winds will likely be shifting and we \nwill be coming out of the lower end of the astronomical high \ntide cycle, only minor tidal flooding is expected in the worst \ncase scenario.\n\nKEY MESSAGE 3...Potential for another storm system by \nThanksgiving.\n\nCooler and dry conditions expected by Tuesday as strong \nCanadian high pressure builds across the northern states and New\nEngland. There is an increasing signal of some type of low \npressure development by Wednesday or Thanksgiving along the \nfrontal boundary south of the area. This is still a week out and\nthere will be plenty of time to pin down any details. However, \ngiven the +2 sigma high to the north, any low pressure to our \nsouth will lead to increased NE winds and the potential for some\ntidal flooding as we will be going into another King Tide \ncycle.",
